Order the amplifier around the codec's power transitions, and drop the idle
grace period.

Traced on Device #1 over a real A2DP session with several track skips:

  32:34  amp=lo  dac=96      idle
  35:27  amp=hi  dac=d6      playback starts
                             <- 39 s, several track skips, ZERO transitions
  36:06  amp=hi  dac=96      playback stops, DAC down, amplifier still up
  36:16  amp=lo  dac=96      amplifier off, exactly 10 s later

Two things follow.

First, the per-track pop does not exist in the real playback path.
bluealsa-aplay holds the ALSA PCM open for the whole A2DP session, so DAPM
never tears the DAC down between tracks - the same thing Android's HAL does on
stock with its standby timeout. The per-track pops chased in r185 and r186 were
an artefact of testing with repeated aplay invocations, which open and close
the PCM every time.

Second, the ten-second gap between the DAC powering down and the amplifier
switching off is the idle grace period added in r185, and it is exactly the
whine the user hears: the amplifier is up while its input is undriven. The
grace period buys nothing in the real path and costs this, so it goes.

The amplifier is instead ordered around the codec:

  - ON is delayed by MT8163_BISCUIT_AMP_ON_DELAY_MS. DAPM powers the DAC up
    after .startup returns, so enabling the amplifier there left it live across
    the DAC's power-up transient, which is the pop at the start of playback.
  - OFF happens in .shutdown, which runs before snd_soc_dapm_stream_stop(), so
    the amplifier is already off when the DAC powers down and nothing is left
    amplifying an undriven input.

The dac_hold() force-enable from r186 is removed. It could never have worked:
snd_soc_dapm_force_enable_pin() only sets w->connected, and DAPM clears
w->is_ep on the DAI widget at SND_SOC_DAPM_STREAM_STOP (soc-dapm.c), so the
endpoint test (is_ep & dir) && connected fails and the chain loses its input
endpoint regardless. It also forced HPL, which this board never uses - the
device is mono on the right path, and HPL, HPL Power, HPL Output Mixer and
Left DAC all read Off even mid-stream.

Trade-off, stated plainly: userspace that opens and closes the PCM per track -
plain aplay, not bluealsa-aplay - gets an amplifier transition per track again.
Both transitions are now correctly ordered against the DAC, so they are much
quieter than the r183 behaviour they resemble, and neither A2DP nor the 3.5 mm
jack is affected.

